## 2 New contradictions

2a Against rulings: none found. C-10's conditions match B′/OQ-17 D verbatim; C-15 matches R-82.a (3); the may_remove default `nobody` matches OQ-12; IC-2 matches OQ-14 D‴; the FX-1 "disable" deferral remains behind RA-5/OP-19, the card-sanctioned route; R-67 handling matches the T-3388 ruling. 2b Internal: two minor seams introduced or left by the row-6 fix — see 3a and 3b.

## 3 Policy format (10.3) implementability

3a Signing is now deterministic: canonical RFC 8785 payload, domain-separated signature, recomputation mandated, epochs per (scope, kind), replace-not-merge, and same-level resolution (deny overrides allow, smallest limit, order-independent) are all normative. Two implementations would sign identically. 3b Remaining ambiguity (evaluation): "a rule matching the request" (10.3.7.b) is never defined normatively — whether a broader `where` (e.g. fleet) matches a narrower place, and how relation selectors compare to a concrete target, is implied only. 3c Minor inconsistency: 10.3.4.c says only the four event-verb classes can have a policy route, but the C-3 cell ("policy only if that hub's list … names it"), the 10.2.6 growth path and OP-2 option C all suppose policy routes for classes with no verb; the format cannot express them without a schema change the text does not state. 3d Minor inconsistency: the 10.3.8 defaults table carries `be_main`, which is not one of the fourteen closed verbs (10.3.4.a); a `may: "be_main"` rule would be refused by 10.3.2 yet defaults-table readers may treat it as valid.

## 4 Verdict

**Confirmed after listed fixes.** Fix list: 4a1 define the rule-matching predicate for `who`/`toward`/`where` normatively (3b); 4a2 state that policy routes for classes without an event verb require a schema extension, and align the C-3 cell, 10.2.6 and OP-2 C with it (3c); 4a3 remove the `be_main` row from 10.3.8 or make it explicitly the profile default, not a verb (3d).
